   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7e922cba-0e74-4190-8f78-b3b9e055b2c7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Making Carbonate Sensitivities for ROMS-MARBL-LiTE\n",
    "This notebook demonstrates how to make the carbonate sensitivities `eta` and `beta`, needed to run `ROMS` with `CDR-Lite` (see how to conduct a CDR release with `CDR-LiTE` [here](https://roms-tools.readthedocs.io/en/latest/cdr_forcing.html#Using-CDR-releases-with-CDR-LiTE-(non-MARBL-BGC))). Rather than running the full biogeochemical model `MARBL`, `CDR-LiTE` requires only a timeseries of the `eta` and `beta` fields. Where,\n",
    "- ``eta  = dDIC / dALK``\n",
    "- ``beta = dDIC / dCO2``\n",
    "\n",
    "`eta` and `beta` can optionally be output from a previous run of `ROMS-MARBL` by setting `do_cdr_gas_exch_output` to `.true.` in the namelist. Alternatively, we can calculate `eta` and `beta`, using `bgc` and `his` files from a previous run of `ROMS-MARBL`. We demonstrate the latter in this notebook. The variables collected from these files are `temp` and `salt` from the `his` files, and `ALK_ALT_CO2`, `DIC_ALT_CO2`, `PO4`, `SiO3` from the `bgc` files. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Note:** the frequency of output of your `bgc` and `his` records will determine how frequently your `eta` and `beta` fields are updated in a `ROMS-CDR-LiTE` simulation. In this example we use daily.\n",
    "\n",
    "The `his` and `bgc` files should be instantaneous (snapshot) output, not averages, and their records should span the whole `ROMS-MARBL-LiTE` run: `ROMS` needs a record at or before the run's start and one at or after its end. Land cells, and cells below `MARBL`'s salinity, alkalinity and DIC floors, are set to 0.\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "b9e455f1-e3a6-4e0d-b733-6fd4e9f9e432",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from roms_tools import Grid, calculate_eta_beta"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "370ace3e-9f54-491d-982d-1e632c96348a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Here we calculate `eta` and `beta` for a fjord in Iceland"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "0c0466cf-0dfe-4e17-a61f-9d534c504dde",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
